**Husky** makes **Git hooks** easy: commands that run automatically before a commit or when you write its message. With it you can stop unformatted code, lint errors or messy commit messages from getting into the repository.

In this note we'll set up, in this order:

1. **Prettier**, to format code.
2. **ESLint**, to catch errors.
3. **Husky**, to run both before every commit.
4. **commitlint**, to enforce [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org).

> This guide uses **Husky v9**. If you see tutorials with `npx husky-init` or `npx husky add`, they're for older versions: those commands no longer work.

## 1. Prettier

Install it:

```bash
npm install --save-dev --save-exact prettier
```

Create a `.prettierrc` config file at the project root. This is the one I use:

```json
{
  "printWidth": 80,
  "semi": true,
  "singleQuote": true,
  "tabWidth": 2,
  "trailingComma": "all",
  "endOfLine": "auto"
}
```

To format the whole project, or just check without changing files:

```bash
npx prettier --write .
npx prettier --check .
```

Prettier ignores whatever is in `.gitignore`. If you need to ignore more, list it in a `.prettierignore` file.

## 2. ESLint

ESLint has a wizard that creates the config for you:

```bash
npm init @eslint/config@latest
```

Answer the questions (JavaScript or TypeScript, React or not, and so on) and it creates an `eslint.config.mjs` file. To check the project:

```bash
npx eslint .
```

So ESLint and Prettier don't fight over formatting, install `eslint-config-prettier`:

```bash
npm install --save-dev eslint-config-prettier
```

And add it **last** in your `eslint.config.mjs`:

```js
import eslintConfigPrettier from 'eslint-config-prettier';

export default [
  // ...your current config
  eslintConfigPrettier,
];
```

Finally, add two scripts to your `package.json`:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "format": "prettier --write . && eslint --fix .",
    "lint": "prettier --check . && eslint ."
  }
}
```

## 3. Husky

You need a Git repository that's already initialized (`git init`). Install Husky and run its init command:

```bash
npm install --save-dev husky
npx husky init
```

`husky init` does two things:

- Creates the `.husky/` folder with a `pre-commit` hook.
- Adds the `"prepare": "husky"` script to your `package.json`, so hooks install automatically when someone runs `npm install`.

Now edit `.husky/pre-commit` so it runs your lint script. The file should contain just this line:

```bash
npm run lint
```

From now on, if the code doesn't pass Prettier or ESLint, the commit is cancelled.

> On a large project, checking everything on every commit can be slow. With [lint-staged](https://github.com/lint-staged/lint-staged) you can check only the files you're committing.

## 4. commitlint and Conventional Commits

Install commitlint with the Conventional Commits config:

```bash
npm install --save-dev @commitlint/cli @commitlint/config-conventional
```

Create `commitlint.config.mjs` at the root:

```js
export default { extends: ['@commitlint/config-conventional'] };
```

And create the hook that checks every commit message, in `.husky/commit-msg`:

```bash
npx --no -- commitlint --edit $1
```

## Try it

Try a commit with any message:

```bash
git commit -m "changes"
```

commitlint rejects it because it doesn't follow the format. This one passes:

```bash
git commit -m "feat: add contact form"
```

The most common types are `feat` (new feature), `fix` (bug fix), `docs`, `refactor`, `test` and `chore`.

## Summary

| Tool | What it does | Where it's configured |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Prettier | Formats code | `.prettierrc` |
| ESLint | Catches errors | `eslint.config.mjs` |
| Husky | Runs commands on every commit | `.husky/pre-commit`, `.husky/commit-msg` |
| commitlint | Checks the commit message | `commitlint.config.mjs` |

If you also want a clean history before a pull request, see [how to squash commits in Git](/en/notes/how-to-make-a-squash-in-git).
